  dat <- reactive({
    con %>%
      tbl("mytable"))
  })

  output$tbl <- DT::renderDataTable({
    dat()
  })
}

shinyApp(ui, server)
```

I'm sure this has been encountered by the community. When a variable contains decimal values, the column filter always add 15 decimal points. For example:

    data <- data.frame(z =  c(0.0624, 0.0233, 0.0176, 0.0110, 0.0106, 0.0103))
    DT::datatable(data, filter="top")

leads to the column filter (0.010300000000000 , 0.06240000001)

This is because how `decimals` function in `datatable()` is defined. 

In DT/R/datatables.R line 373 - 329

     decimals = function(x) {
        x = abs(na.omit(x))
        if (length(x) == 0) return()
        i = 0L
        while (i < 15 && any(round(x, i) != x)) i = i + 1L
        if (i > 0L) i
      }

where the intention is to stop increasing `i` when rounded values are equal to the value itself. But because of floating point issue, this often counts up to 15. One specific example I encountered is when a variable column is loaded into R via `library(RMySQL)`, the above code correctly counts the decimal points. However, If I divide that variable by 100 or do some sort of operations on it, then the `decimal` function treats the variable differently and counts up to 15.

Changing the above to:

    decimals = function(x) {
        x = abs(na.omit(x))
        if (length(x) == 0) return()
        i = 0L
        while (i < 15 && !isTRUE(all.equal(round(x, i), x))) i = i + 1L
        return(i)
    }

seems to solve the issue.

